OpenFlux for Routerich AX3000

Activation script revision: r2.2.

Current connection remains VK TURN. Its /etc/vkturn directory and routing table 200 are preserved.

Activate (from Windows PowerShell):
ssh -t root@192.168.1.1 "sh /etc/openflux/activate.sh"

The script requires three consecutive SOCKS + TUN checks before switching LAN/DNS, then asks for YES.
Check websites and https://api.ipify.org on a LAN device: expected IP 37.252.5.64.
Type YES within the printed confirmation timeout (up to 120 seconds) to keep OpenFlux as primary and enable autostart.
The persistent routing marker is saved only after YES. Boot/restart restores OpenFlux LAN policy only after three successful TUN checks.
Any other answer, timeout or interrupted SSH restores VK TURN.
Rollback removes the OpenFlux policy immediately, before waiting for its processes to stop.
Failed preflight stops the isolated OpenFlux client and leaves the VK TURN policy in place.
An independent guard restores VK TURN after 180 seconds from cutover if the activation shell disappears. A transient post-switch TUN probe is retried before rollback.
VK TURN is not stopped or removed, including after confirmation.

Immediate rollback (from Windows PowerShell):
ssh root@192.168.1.1 "sh /etc/openflux/restore-vkturn.sh"

Read-only readiness check while OpenFlux is running:
sh /etc/openflux/activate.sh --check

Yandex captcha/login on the router:
If logread reports "yandex docs: captcha required", the Yandex carrier cannot connect.
A handshake timeout then prevents the local SOCKS listener from starting. Increasing the
activation delay does not solve this authentication failure.
The core supports a Netscape cookies.txt export for vyandex. Sign in to Yandex and open
the configured editable document in a browser. Export only the relevant Yandex cookies
locally; keep this file out of Git, chat, and diagnostic output.
Copy it from Windows PowerShell (replace the local path):
scp -O "C:/path/to/yandex-cookies.txt" root@192.168.1.1:/etc/openflux/yandex-cookies.txt
ssh root@192.168.1.1 "chmod 600 /etc/openflux/yandex-cookies.txt"
The next manual activation automatically passes this file via --yandex-cookies-file.
With an explicit browser export, the core uses a temporary JSON store reset at each
service start so stale cached cookies cannot overwrite the imported login. The original
/etc/openflux/cookies.json stays intact. Updated runtime cookies are temporary; a later
captcha/login wall can require refreshing the browser export.
Uploading the file does not start a service or switch routing. No restart is needed
before the manual activation command above. The normal readiness checks still apply.
Browser cookies obtained through VK TURN may still be challenged when reused from the
router's WAN address. If captcha persists, solve it from that same WAN address and
refresh the export; the presence of a cookie file alone does not prove authentication.

Isolated CAPTCHA browser through the router WAN (Windows):
Run from the repository with its existing Python environment and installed Chrome:
.venv/Scripts/python.exe scripts/openflux-router/authorize-yandex.py
Enter the router SSH password in the local masked dialog. The tool opens an empty
Chrome context through a loopback-only SSH HTTP proxy; its Yandex connections use
IPv4 through the router, with the pinned core's user-agent. It imports no personal
browser cookies. Pass the check without signing into a Yandex account, then click
the local Save button. Only unexpired yandex.ru cookies are sent to the router;
account-session cookies are explicitly rejected and values/URLs are never logged.
The proxy accepts only HTTPS Yandex destinations. It and the temporary browser close
on completion, cancellation, or the 15-minute interaction deadline. The previous
router cookie export is preserved as yandex-cookies.before-wan-auth.txt (root-only).
The browser can be let through without a visible CAPTCHA or a spravka cookie. Saving
requires that the editor page is open and that the user clicks Save; it stages the
fresh anonymous WAN browser session for the next manual activation. Requests made
with curl can be challenged even when the browser opens the editor; that is not a
readiness test for OpenFlux. The tool never starts OpenFlux or changes routing.
Manually activate and use the normal SOCKS/TUN preflight checks afterward. No tunnel
or full-LAN readiness is assumed merely because the browser opens the document.

OpenFlux: /tmp/openflux-core, node-v1.3.1 arm64; verified gzip stored in /etc/openflux/openflux.gz.
Private key and config stay under /etc/openflux, root-only.
Existing sing-box supplies oflux0 (198.19.0.1/30, MTU 1280), SOCKS5 is localhost:1080.
New routing table 201 overrides LAN/DNS only while activated. Router bootstrap traffic stays on WAN.
Yandex transport and encrypted direct fallback reuse the installed VPS profile at 37.252.5.64:8445.
Existing IPv6 forwarding guard remains because the tunnel routes IPv4.
No router reboot was performed.

These source files contain no private channel key, document address or account cookies.
The installed VPS profile in /etc/openflux/client.conf and encryption.key is kept separately.

Diagnostic 2026-11-11: Yandex passed isolated HTTPS and UDP DNS, but 6/16 concurrent TUN HTTPS requests failed. Full-LAN reliability has not been established. VK TURN remains the default; OpenFlux autostart is off until an explicit successful cutover and YES.
